\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}

\taxon{Example}
\title{Flags of a triangle and a tetrahedron}

\p{The face-poset construction of \citet{Section 2}{schulte2017regular}
gives the following elementary examples of \vocabk{flags}{frap-000D}.
A triangle has the empty face, three vertices, three edges and the whole
triangle, of ranks #{-1,0,1,2}. The flag
#{\varnothing<1<12<123} has rank-zero neighbor
#{\varnothing<2<12<123} and rank-one neighbor
#{\varnothing<1<13<123}. There are #{3\cdot2=6} flags.}

\tikzfig2{\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\small}]
\node (a) at (0,1.5) {$(1,12)$};
\node (b) at (2,1.5) {$(2,12)$};
\node (c) at (3,0) {$(2,23)$};
\node (d) at (2,-1.5) {$(3,23)$};
\node (e) at (0,-1.5) {$(3,13)$};
\node (f) at (-1,0) {$(1,13)$};
\draw (a) -- node[above] {$0$} (b);
\draw (b) -- node[right] {$1$} (c);
\draw (c) -- node[right] {$0$} (d);
\draw (d) -- node[below] {$1$} (e);
\draw (e) -- node[left] {$0$} (f);
\draw (f) -- node[left] {$1$} (a);
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{Each vertex in this flag graph records the vertex and edge of one flag;
the common empty and greatest faces are suppressed. Edge labels give the
rank changed by adjacency.}

\p{A tetrahedron has all subsets of #{\{1,2,3,4\}} as \vocabk{faces}{frap-000D}, ordered by
inclusion; a nonempty face with #{k} vertices has rank #{k-1}. Its flag
#{\varnothing<1<12<123<1234} exhibits all five ranks. The
  \vocabk{vertex figure}{frap-000E} at #{1} is a triangle: its vertices correspond
to the three incident edges. A cube also has a triangular vertex figure,
although its facets are squares. Local and global shapes carry different
information.}
